#
# The ext/ boundary, measured from BusyBox ash's side — and it is not dash's
# boundary.
#
# dash and ash both sit outside ext/ in docs/spec/shell-matrix.md, and the
# two columns' Dirs say so identically. This file is why that identical
# spelling hides two different shells: BusyBox ash has the condition command,
# `$'…'`, the `function` keyword and process substitution, and dash has none
# of the four. What they share is the refusal of arrays, here strings, the
# C-style for and `+=`.
#
# So "ash does not run ext/" is a coarser statement than it looks, and this
# is the fine-grained version of it. Each construct ext/ has a file for is
# put to ash and the answer recorded: which ones it takes, which it refuses,
# and at what status.
#
# Each is inside `( eval … )` for dash/boundary.tests' reason: a parse error
# is fatal to the shell it is in, and the subshell is what lets the file
# reach its next line.

# Arrays — refused, as in dash. ext/arrays.tests.
( eval 'a=(one two)' ) 2>&1
printf 'array: %s\n' "$?"

# The condition command — taken, where dash has not got it.
# ext/dblbracket.tests.
( eval '[[ a == a ]] && echo condition-ran' ) 2>&1
printf 'double bracket: %s\n' "$?"

# $'…' — taken, and the escapes processed. dash reads the same word as a
# literal dollar in front of a single-quoted string.
# ext/dollarquote.tests.
( eval "printf '%s\n' \$'a\tb'" ) 2>&1
printf 'dollar quote: %s\n' "$?"

# The `function` keyword — taken. ext/funcform.tests.
( eval 'function g { echo function-body-ran; }; g' ) 2>&1
printf 'function keyword: %s\n' "$?"

# Process substitution — taken. ext/procsub.tests.
( eval 'cat <(echo substituted)' ) 2>&1
printf 'process substitution: %s\n' "$?"

# Here strings — refused, as in dash. ext/herestring.tests.
( eval 'cat <<< word' ) 2>&1
printf 'here string: %s\n' "$?"

# The C-style for loop — refused, as in dash. ext/cfor.tests.
( eval 'for ((i=0;i<1;i++)); do echo c; done' ) 2>&1
printf 'C-style for: %s\n' "$?"

# select — refused. ext/select.tests.
( eval 'select x in a; do break; done < /dev/null' ) 2>&1
printf 'select: %s\n' "$?"

# `+=` — not refused and not implemented: with no append operator the word is
# a command name, so the shell goes looking for a program called `v+=y` and
# the assignment never happens. Same misreading as dash's.
# ext/plusassign.tests.
( eval 'v=x; v+=y; echo $v' ) 2>&1
printf 'append: %s\n' "$?"

# Pattern substitution and substrings — taken, where dash refuses both. They
# are ext/patsub.tests and ext/substrings.tests, and they are the two rows
# that put ash and dash on opposite sides while one Dirs list covers them.
v=abc
printf '%s\n' "${v/b/B}"
printf '%s\n' "${v//b/B}"
printf '%s\n' "${v:1:1}"
printf '%s\n' "${v:1}"

# Exponentiation — taken, where dash refuses it.
printf '%s\n' "$((2**3))"
